IMPORT prelude

-- Widows' and Orphans' Pension Act 1904 (Singapore) -- who contributes and for how long (ss 2, 4, 5), what a bachelor or widower
-- gets back on retiring, dying or being removed (ss 12-14), the family notices and the $25 fine (ss 22, 23), which widows are
-- shut out (s 25), when a widow's pension stops (s 27), how the pension is split between a widow and the children of earlier
-- marriages (ss 30-34, 38), adopted children (s 39), and the arithmetic of the First Schedule's worked examples (Rules I, VI, IX, X).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/WOPA1904.txt. The deposit says the edition
-- "incorporates all amendments up to and including 1 December 2021 and comes into operation on 31 December 2021"; the latest
-- amendment annotated in the body is [8/1995] (the Pension Fund Act 1995), and the Legislative History ends with Act 8 of 1995.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Requirement REQ-0226 in subjects/sg/requirements.jsonl: Tier 3 of the remaining Singapore Acts, ordered by
-- everyday-life relevance. The requirement asks what the Act decides for a person it applies to; no scenario has asked a
-- sharper question yet. The Act is closed to new entrants in practice: s 2 excludes from "public servant" anyone appointed to a
-- pensionable office on or after 2 March 1961, so its live questions are those of surviving widows and children of older officers.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: the one-off options of ss 7 and 8 (entitled officers; the 1965 election), reduced-income and ill-health
-- contributions (ss 9-11), transfers abroad and to Malaysia (ss 15-20), the Crown Agents rules (s 21), sterling salaries
-- (ss 4(3), 24(3)), the register itself (s 22(1)), proof of life (s 40), minors' payees (s 41), the bar on assignment (s 42), the
-- President's final decision (s 43), the pre-1955 saving (s 44), Rules II to V and F, and Tables A, B and C themselves -- the
-- factors read from the tables are passed in as numbers, taken from the Schedule's own worked examples.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Widows' and Orphans' Pension Act 1904 -- pensions`

§§ `2 and 29 -- of a pensionable age`

-- s 2: "of a pensionable age", as applied to children, means, "in the case of a male, that he is under the age of 21 years, and,
-- in the case of a female, that she is under the age of 21 years and has not been married".
-- s 29: children's pensions cease, for a male, at 21, and for a female, at 21 "or marrying under that age".
GIVEN `is male` IS A BOOLEAN, `age in years` IS A NUMBER, `has been married` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the child is of a pensionable age` IF
    `age in years` LESS THAN 21 AND (`is male` OR (NOT `has been married`))

§§ `4 and 5 -- the monthly abatement and how long it runs`

-- s 4(1): "A monthly abatement shall be made from the salary or pension of every public servant"; (2) "calculated at the rate
-- of 4% of the monthly salary of the contributor"; (4) contributions continue "on the full salary" during half-pay or no-pay leave.
-- s 5(1): the abatement continues "until the contributor attains the age of 65 years, if he continues so long to draw salary or
-- pension from the Government, or until such abatement has been made for 35 successive years ... in either of which events such
-- abatement shall cease". (2): on the salary of the permanent appointment, not acting pay or (non-pensionable) allowances.
GIVEN `full monthly salary` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the monthly abatement` MEANS `full monthly salary` TIMES 4 DIVIDED BY 100

GIVEN `age attained` IS A NUMBER, `years of abatement made` IS A NUMBER, `still draws salary or pension` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the abatement continues` IF
        `still draws salary or pension`
    AND `age attained` LESS THAN 65
    AND `years of abatement made` LESS THAN 35

§§ `12, 13 and 14 -- leaving the service`

-- s 12(1): a married contributor, or a widower with children of a pensionable age, who retires before he is entitled to a pension
-- "may continue to contribute"; (2) six months in arrear and he is treated as having ceased.
-- s 13(1): a bachelor or a widower without pensionable children who retires "shall cease to contribute and his rights under this
-- Act shall cease", except that (2), on retiring or dying in service, there is payable to him or his legal representative (a) for
-- a bachelor, "the total amount of his contributions together with compound interest thereon calculated with annual rests at the
-- rate of 2½%"; (b) for such a widower, the contributions paid "since the death of his last wife or the ceasing to be of pensionable
-- age of his last child", with the same interest.
-- s 14: a contributor "removed from the service" ceases to contribute; (a) and (b) the same refunds, "subject to the deduction of
-- any sums due by him to the Government"; (c) if married or a widower with pensionable children, his widow or children take a
-- pension on "the pension rights acquired ... at the date of the cessation of his contributions".
DECLARE Family IS ONE OF
    `a bachelor`
    `a widower without pensionable children`
    `married`
    `a widower with pensionable children`

DECLARE Departure IS ONE OF
    `retires before earning a pension`
    `dies in service`
    `is removed from the service`

DECLARE Outcome IS ONE OF
    `all contributions refunded with interest`
    `contributions since the last wife or child ceased refunded with interest`
    `he may go on contributing`
    `pension for widow or children on rights already acquired`
    `pension for widow or children under ss 30 to 38`

GIVEN `the family` IS A Family, `the departure` IS A Departure
GIVETH AN Outcome
`what follows` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the family`
    WHEN `a bachelor` THEN `all contributions refunded with interest`
    WHEN `a widower without pensionable children` THEN `contributions since the last wife or child ceased refunded with interest`
    OTHERWISE
        CONSIDER `the departure`
        WHEN `retires before earning a pension` THEN `he may go on contributing`
        WHEN `is removed from the service` THEN `pension for widow or children on rights already acquired`
        OTHERWISE `pension for widow or children under ss 30 to 38`

-- Compound interest "with annual rests at the rate of 2½%", for one sum left for a whole number of years.
GIVEN `sum` IS A NUMBER, `whole years` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`compounded at 2.5%` MEANS
    IF `whole years` AT MOST 0
    THEN `sum`
    ELSE `compounded at 2.5%` (`sum` TIMES 1.025) (`whole years` MINUS 1)

-- s 14(a): the removed bachelor's refund, less any sums he owes the Government. (On retirement, s 13(2) makes no such deduction.)
GIVEN `contributions` IS A NUMBER, `years held` IS A NUMBER, `owed to the Government` IS A NUMBER, `was removed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the bachelor's refund` MEANS
    IF `was removed`
    THEN (`compounded at 2.5%` `contributions` `years held`) MINUS `owed to the Government`
    ELSE `compounded at 2.5%` `contributions` `years held`

§§ `22 and 23 -- family notices and the fine`

-- s 22(2): a public servant entering the service must "within 6 months" notify his date of birth and, if married, his marriage
-- and the births of his wife and children. (3): a marriage, or an annulment or dissolution, "within 3 months". (4): the birth of
-- each child, the marriage of a daughter under 21, and the death of his wife or a pensionable child, each within 3 months. (5):
-- after his death the WIDOW must give notice of the death, a posthumous birth, a daughter's marriage or a child's death within
-- 3 months.
-- s 23(1): a contributor or widow who fails "shall for each default pay a fine not exceeding $25", deducted from salary or
-- pension. (2): a wilful false statement may forfeit "all or any part of his rights", at the Directors' discretion.
DECLARE Notifiable IS ONE OF
    `entering the service`
    `his marriage`
    `a decree ending his marriage`
    `the birth of a child`
    `a daughter under 21 marrying`
    `the death of his wife or a pensionable child`
    `his own death, notified by the widow`

GIVEN `the event` IS A Notifiable
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the months allowed` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the event`
    WHEN `entering the service` THEN 6
    OTHERWISE 3

GIVEN `defaults` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the most that can be deducted` MEANS `defaults` TIMES 25

§§ `25 -- widows who take nothing`

-- s 25(1): not entitled are "(a) a widow whose marriage was contracted after her husband had completed his 35 years of
-- contributions or attained the age of 65 years or retired or been removed from the service"; (b) "the issue of such a marriage";
-- (c) "a widow whose husband dies within 12 calendar months of the marriage without issue of such marriage born in his lifetime or
-- in due time after his death". (2): the President "may, if he thinks fit", allow the (c) widow all or part of the pension.
GIVEN `married after he stopped contributing or left the service` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `he died within 12 months of the marriage` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `a child of the marriage was born` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the President allows it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the widow may receive a pension` IF
        (NOT `married after he stopped contributing or left the service`)
    AND (    (NOT `he died within 12 months of the marriage`)
         OR  `a child of the marriage was born`
         OR  `the President allows it`)

§§ `27 -- when a widow's pension ceases`

-- s 27(1): "A widow's pension shall cease on her death or remarriage or on her becoming a bankrupt." (2): she is then "deemed to
-- have died", so the children take (s 35); but if it ceased for bankruptcy the President may direct payment to her of the part not
-- payable to children. Living abroad does not end it; s 40 only lets payment be refused until proof of life is produced.
DECLARE WidowEvent IS ONE OF
    `she dies`
    `she remarries`
    `she becomes a bankrupt`
    `she moves abroad`
    `she takes a job`

GIVEN `the widow event` IS A WidowEvent
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the widow's pension ceases` IF
    CONSIDER `the widow event`
    WHEN `she moves abroad` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `she takes a job` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `30 to 34 and 38 -- splitting the pension`

-- s 30: a widow with no pensionable child by a previous marriage takes her full pension.
-- s 31: no widow, children by one marriage: they take their mother's pension. s 32: no widow, children by 2 or more marriages:
-- each marriage's children take "one-half, one-third or one-quarter, as the case may be", of their mother's pension.
-- s 33: a widow and children by ONE previous marriage: each side takes one-half. s 34: a widow and children by 2 or more previous
-- marriages: "one-third or one-quarter, as the case may be". s 38: children of a marriage "take the same in equal shares".
-- A widow's own children take nothing while she draws (s 35 gives them her pension when she ceases).
-- INFERENCE: "as the case may be" is read as one share per claiming line (the widow, and each earlier marriage with pensionable
-- children). The text names no fraction below one-quarter, so five or more lines are not covered by the words.
GIVEN `the widow is alive and entitled` IS A BOOLEAN, `earlier marriages with pensionable children` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of lines` MEANS
    IF `the widow is alive and entitled`
    THEN `earlier marriages with pensionable children` PLUS 1
    ELSE `earlier marriages with pensionable children`

GIVEN `the widow is alive and entitled` IS A BOOLEAN, `earlier marriages with pensionable children` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`each line's fraction` MEANS
    1 DIVIDED BY (`the number of lines` `the widow is alive and entitled` `earlier marriages with pensionable children`)

-- Rule VI(a) and s 38: the pension is divided "equally among the children entitled for the time being".
GIVEN `the line's pension` IS A NUMBER, `children entitled now` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`each child's pension` MEANS `the line's pension` DIVIDED BY `children entitled now`

§§ `39 -- adopted children`

-- s 39(1): a child adopted "while he is married to any wife" is deemed a child of that marriage if (a) adopted "before he retired
-- from the public service"; (b) "the contributor was under the age of 55 years at the date of adoption"; and (c) the adoption is
-- registered under the Adoption of Children Act 1939 or otherwise valid where he was domiciled or resident.
GIVEN `adopted while married` IS A BOOLEAN, `adopted before he retired` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `his age at adoption` IS A NUMBER, `the adoption is valid` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the adopted child counts` IF
        `adopted while married`
    AND `adopted before he retired`
    AND `his age at adoption` LESS THAN 55
    AND `the adoption is valid`

§§ `First Schedule -- the worked examples`

-- Rule I(a): "Accumulate the contributions at 8% compound interest, with yearly rests at each 31 December, and multiply the result
-- by the quantity found from Table A". The example adds half a year's interest at 8% for a part year. The Schedule rounds to the
-- cent at each step; this encoding does not round, so results are compared to within a cent.
GIVEN `balance` IS A NUMBER, `paid this year` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`a year on at 8%` MEANS (`balance` TIMES 108 DIVIDED BY 100) PLUS `paid this year`

GIVEN `balance` IS A NUMBER, `paid this half year` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`half a year on at 8%` MEANS (`balance` TIMES 104 DIVIDED BY 100) PLUS `paid this half year`

-- Rule I(a), I(b), I(c): pension = amount x the table quantity (accumulation x Table A; annual contribution, or its increment or
-- decrement, x Table B).
GIVEN `amount` IS A NUMBER, `table quantity` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the pension it buys` MEANS `amount` TIMES `table quantity`

-- Rule IX: the s 7(1)(b) single contribution is "the annual contribution ... by the Table B factor and dividing the product by the
-- Table A factor".
GIVEN `annual contribution` IS A NUMBER, `Table B factor` IS A NUMBER, `Table A factor` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the single contribution` MEANS `annual contribution` TIMES `Table B factor` DIVIDED BY `Table A factor`

-- Rule X: the repayment is the prospective pension less "the product of his annual contribution and Table B factor", multiplied
-- by the Table C factor.
GIVEN `prospective pension` IS A NUMBER, `annual contribution` IS A NUMBER, `Table B factor` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the paid-up pension` MEANS `prospective pension` MINUS (`annual contribution` TIMES `Table B factor`)

GIVEN `prospective pension` IS A NUMBER, `annual contribution` IS A NUMBER, `Table B factor` IS A NUMBER, `Table C factor` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the repayment` MEANS
    (`the paid-up pension` `prospective pension` `annual contribution` `Table B factor`) TIMES `Table C factor`

GIVEN `computed` IS A NUMBER, `printed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is within a cent of` IF
    (`computed` MINUS `printed`) LESS THAN 0.01 AND (`printed` MINUS `computed`) LESS THAN 0.01
